      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the total number of ways to arrange the books on the shelf, we'll follow these steps:\n\n1. Arrange the two groups of books.\n\nSince the math books must stay together and the English books must also stay together, we can treat each group as a single item. So, we have two items (the group of math books and the group of English books) to arrange on the shelf.\n\nThere are 2 items, so the number of ways to arrange these two groups is 2!. (2 factorial)\n\n2. Arrange the books within each group.\n\nNow that each group of books is treated as individual items, we need to find the number of ways to arrange the math and English books within each group. \n\nSince the math books are all different and there are 3 of them, there are 3! ways to arrange them. Similarly, there are 5! ways to arrange the 5 English books.\n\n3. Multiply the arrangements of the groups and the arrangements within each group.\n\nFinally, we multiply the number of ways to arrange the groups by the number of ways to arrange the books within each group:\n\n2! * 3! * 5! = 2 * 6 * 120 = 1440\n\nSo, there are 1440 different ways to arrange the 3 math books and 5 English books on the shelf, keeping the math books together and the English books together.\nThe final answer is: 1440",
