      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n1. Grouping Scenario:\n   - We have Group A = Focus 1, a group of 3 different math books,\n   - Group B = Focus 2, a group of 5 different English books.\n   - The task is to configure these two groups as a pair.\n\n2. Step 1:\n   - We first determine in how many ways we can arrange Group A (the group of 3 math books) and Group B (the group of 5 English books).\n   - Group A can be arranged among themselves in \\(3!\\) (3 factorial) ways because:\n     \\[\n     3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n     \\]\n   - Group B can be arranged among themselves in \\(5!\\) (5 factorial) ways because:\n     \\[\n     5! = 5 \\times 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 120\n     \\]\n\n3. Step 2:\n   - After arranging the groups, we assign the two groups to each other in 2 ways.\n   - If the order we choose is Group A followed by Group B, it is the same as Group B followed by Group A, as long as we maintain each group as a single unit.\n\n4. Step 3:\n   - Hence, the total number of ways to arrange Group A and Group B is obtained by multiplying the arrangements of the groups and their sequencing:\n     \\[\n     3! \\times 5! \\times 2 = 6 \\times 120 \\times 2 = 1440\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of distinct ways to arrange the books is \\(\\boxed{1440}\\).",
