      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the number of ways to arrange the books under the given constraints, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Group the Books**: First, note that we have two distinct groups of books: the math books and the English books. Both groups must stay together.\n\n2. **Treat Each Group as a Single Unit**: Consider each group as a single unit. We have two units (one for the math books and one for the English books).\n\n3. **Calculate Irreducible Configurations**: The task reduces to arranging two distinct units on a shelf. The number of ways to arrange two distinct units is given by the factorial of 2, which is \\(2!\\):\n   \\[\n   2! = 2\n   \\]\n\n4. **Arranging Books Within Each Group**: Now, we need to determine the number of ways to arrange the mathematics books and the English books within their respective groups.\n\n- For the math books, there are 3 distinct books. The number of ways to arrange 3 books is \n   \\[\n   3! = 6\n   \\]\n\n- Similarly, for the English books, there are 5 distinct books. The number of ways to arrange 5 books is \n   \\[\n   5! = 120\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate Totals**: To find the total number of arrangements for the defined groups, we combine the configurations step-by-step:\n   \n   - After placing both units, there are \\(2!\\) ways to arrange each unit:\n     \\[\n     2! = 2\n     \\]\n     \n   - The actual rearrangement of the units within all math books and English books:\n     \\[\n     3! \\times 5! = 6 \\times 120 = 720\n     \\]\n     \n   - Then multiply this result by the number of ways to arrange the units that each group can be placed:\n     \\[\n     2 \\times 720 = 1440\n     \\]\n\nPutting it all backward into the format of the problem description:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1440}\n\\]",
